{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"In stable SCOPe 2.08, the catalytic domains belong to three distinct families: canonical protein kinases are “Protein kinases, catalytic subunit” (SUNID 88854; d.144.1.7), aminoglycoside phosphotransferases are “APH phosphotransferases” (SUNID 64411; d.144.1.6), and phosphoinositide 3-kinases are “PI3K, catalytic domain” (SUNID 56171; d.144.1.4). All three families descend from the same Protein kinase-like superfamily (SUNID 56112; d.144.1), within the Protein kinase-like fold (SUNID 56111; d.144) and alpha-and-beta class (SUNID 53931; d). Their shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry of the classified catalytic domains, not merely a similar fold. Combined with their established kinase activities, this is consistent with an evolutionarily conserved, broad ATP-dependent phosphoryl-transfer architecture; SCOPe hierarchy alone does not establish identical catalytic residues or detailed transition-state chemistry.

Because they are separate families, substrate and regulatory properties are unsafe to transfer: protein-substrate recognition from canonical kinases does not establish aminoglycoside or phosphoinositide recognition; aminoglycoside specificity does not transfer to protein or lipid kinases; and PI3K lipid specificity, membrane recruitment, or regulation does not transfer to the other families. Nor does the shared superfamily justify transferring inhibitor sensitivity, kinetic parameters, activation mechanisms, docking motifs, regulatory phosphorylation, partner dependence, oligomeric state, or whole-protein function. A structure from another family may guide hypotheses about the conserved core, but is not automatically an interchangeable template for substrate-binding loops, active-site geometry, insertions, or regulatory interfaces.

Substrate-specific transfer requires domain-level sequence and structural alignments showing corresponding catalytic and specificity residues, preferably substrate/cofactor-bound structures, plus biochemical kinetics, binding measurements, and mutagenesis with the relevant protein, aminoglycoside, or phosphoinositide substrates. Mechanistic claims require comparison of active-site geometry, metal/cofactor usage, and reaction chemistry. Regulatory transfer additionally needs full-length domain-context and boundary evidence, regulatory-domain and interface structures, conformational-state data, post-translational-modification evidence, partner or membrane-interaction experiments, and appropriate cellular assays. Phylogenetic and primary-literature evidence is needed to reconstruct how these family-specific functions and regulatory systems evolved.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info — /api/v1/; /api/v1/releases/current","mcp__scope__search_scop — /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=canonical+protein+kinases&mode=auto&limit=25; /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=aminoglycoside+phosphotransferases&mode=auto&limit=25; /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=phosphatidylinositol+3-kinase+catalytic&mode=auto&limit=20","mcp__scope__get_scop_node — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111/homology"]}]}
